      <div class="rec-content">
        <h4>Deploy Off-Peak Load Shifting with Incentive Pricing <span class="rec-priority priority-high">HIGH PRIORITY</span></h4>
        <p>Peak sessions score 2.8× worse on the optimization reward metric (−19.8 vs −7.1) and carry 2.43× the station load (84 vs 35 kW). Introduce a time-of-use pricing discount of 20–30% during Off-Peak windows. Since price currently has zero correlation with demand (r = −0.01) or reward (r = 0.005), pricing reforms will create a new behavioral signal. Target: shift 10–15% of Peak sessions to Off-Peak, which could improve the mean network reward from −11.8 toward −9.5.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-item">
      <div class="rec-num">2</div>
      <div class="rec-content">
        <h4>Create "Green Charging" Premium Off-Peak Slots with Renewable Incentive <span class="rec-priority priority-high">HIGH PRIORITY</span></h4>
        <p>Off-Peak windows offer 39.3% renewable ratio vs 29.0% during Peak — a 35% relative improvement in clean energy. Sessions charging when renewable ratio ≥ 50% average a reward of −3.16 (5.2× better than low-renewable sessions at −16.4). Introduce a "Green Charging" product tier that explicitly schedules sessions during high-renewable windows. Fleet operators (Buses: 2,760 sessions) are the highest-leverage target for bulk off-peak scheduling agreements.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-item">
      <div class="rec-num">3</div>
      <div class="rec-content">
        <h4>Investigate Highway Off-Peak Queue Anomaly — Right-Size Capacity <span class="rec-priority priority-medium">MEDIUM PRIORITY</span></h4>
        <p>Highway Off-Peak shows the highest queue lengths in the network (4.70 vehicles avg), higher than Peak (4.47). This is counterintuitive and suggests that while load is low, the number of simultaneously arriving vehicles exceeds stationed charger availability. Consider deploying 1–2 additional charger bays at high-utilization Highway stations (ST013, ST001) to absorb off-peak demand surges, which is the very window being targeted for load shift. Failure to expand capacity there would create a bottleneck that defeats the load-shifting strategy.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-item">
      <div class="rec-num">4</div>
      <div class="rec-content">
        <h4>Couple Dynamic Pricing to Real-Time Renewable Ratio — Not Just Demand <span class="rec-priority priority-high">HIGH PRIORITY</span></h4>
        <p>Current pricing (range $5–$15, mean ~$9.96) is essentially random with respect to both grid load (r = −0.01) and renewable availability (r = −0.005). The optimization reward is strongly correlated with renewable ratio (r = 0.59) but not with price. Repricing electricity using a renewable-adjusted tariff — lower price when renewable ratio is high, higher when it is low — would align consumer incentives with the optimization objective. This single intervention addresses the most powerful lever identified in the data.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-item">
      <div class="rec-num">5</div>
      <div class="rec-content">
        <h4>Reduce Long-Tail Wait Times via Smart Queue Prioritization <span class="rec-priority priority-medium">MEDIUM PRIORITY</span></h4>
        <p>While median wait time is acceptable at 9 minutes, 5.5% of sessions wait 15+ minutes (up to 28 min max). Implement dynamic queue management that deprioritizes long-duration sessions (Two-Wheelers and Cars with high battery capacity) when queues exceed 6 vehicles, preferring lower-SOC sessions that need urgent charging. Target: reduce P90 wait time from 16 to under 12 minutes. This has outsized customer satisfaction impact for highway transit use cases where predictable wait times are critical.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-item">
      <div class="rec-num">6</div>
      <div class="rec-content">
        <h4>Upgrade 7 kW and 11 kW Chargers at High-Utilization Peak Stations <span class="rec-priority priority-medium">MEDIUM PRIORITY</span></h4>
        <p>The charger power fleet is evenly split across 7/11/22/50 kW tiers. Sessions on 7 kW chargers contribute disproportionately to session duration (energy is independent of power level), occupying a bay for much longer per kWh delivered. At the busiest stations (ST013: 459 sessions, ST001: 443 sessions), replacing 7 kW and 11 kW chargers with 22 kW units would improve bay throughput by 2–3× for the same energy delivered, directly reducing queue length and wait time without adding new bays.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-item">
